<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> In the Preface, what does Nietzsche identify as the central question driving his inquiry?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">The value of our moral values themselves, and what a critique of those values would look like</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Which ethical tradition, Kantian or utilitarian, better captures moral truth</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Whether God exists and what follows morally from the answer</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Which moral principles are best supported by evolutionary psychology</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Nietzsche asks a question moral philosophers usually skip: what is the value of our moral values themselves? Answering it requires a critique of morality, beginning with its origins.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Nietzsche is not choosing between existing moral theories or arguing about God. In the Preface he turns the question back on morality itself.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Nietzsche describes his method as "genealogical." A genealogy of morality, in his sense, involves:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Tracing the historical conditions, power relations, and psychological needs under which moral concepts emerged</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Applying the methods of natural science to ethical questions</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Consulting ancient texts to recover the original meaning of moral terms</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Developing a new moral theory to replace Christianity</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Genealogy traces where moral concepts came from: the historical conditions, power relations, and psychological needs out of which they grew.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A genealogy is a history of descent. Ask what Nietzsche thinks we learn by tracing the conditions in which moral concepts arose.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> Nietzsche criticizes the "English psychologists" (such as Paul Rée) primarily because:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">They write in English rather than German, making their philosophy inaccessible</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">They accept Kant's account of moral duty without sufficient argument</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">They rely too heavily on religious assumptions</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">They trace the origin of moral concepts to altruism and utility while leaving the basic content of Christian morality unquestioned</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Nietzsche credits the English psychologists with seeking historical origins but says they assumed the unegoistic is good and simply explained it by utility. They never questioned the value of the morality they were explaining.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Nietzsche's complaint is about what these psychologists took for granted. Revisit The Genealogical Method: did they question the values they were explaining?</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> A standard philosophical objection to Nietzsche's genealogical project holds that even if our moral concepts originated in ressentiment and power struggles, this does not by itself show those concepts are false or untrustworthy. This objection is sometimes called:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">The genetic fallacy: the origin of a belief is logically independent of its truth</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">The problem of induction</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">The naturalistic fallacy</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">The is/ought gap</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The genetic fallacy is judging a belief by its origin rather than by its truth or justification. Critics say that even ugly origins would not by themselves show our values are wrong.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The objection concerns the relation between where a belief came from and whether it is true. Ask which fallacy names that confusion.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> In Nietzsche's account of master morality, "good" is originally:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">A religious term inherited from Jewish monotheism</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">A term the powerful use to describe themselves: strong, vital, noble, life-affirming</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">A term of moral condemnation applied to the weak</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">A term meaning "useful to the community," later detached from that origin</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. In master morality, 'good' starts as the nobles' self-description. The powerful call themselves good, and the term expresses their own strength and vitality.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">In master morality, 'good' comes first, and it is something the nobles say about themselves. Ask who is doing the naming.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> In master morality's "good/bad" vocabulary, the term "bad" refers to:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Whatever produces more pain than pleasure in the long run</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Moral wickedness deserving condemnation and punishment</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Actions that violate the categorical imperative</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Social and aesthetic inferiority: weak, common, lowly, without distinction, but carrying no moral condemnation</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For the nobles, 'bad' (schlecht) just means common, low, and inferior. It is a judgment of rank, not of moral wickedness.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Master morality's 'bad' is not the same as slave morality's 'evil.' Ask whether it carries condemnation or simply marks lower status.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> The key structural difference between master morality and slave morality is that:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Master morality is rational while slave morality is emotional</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Master morality begins with self-affirmation ("we are good") and derives "they are bad" as an afterthought; slave morality begins by negating the enemy ("they are evil") and derives "we are good" as a reaction</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Master morality applies only to aristocrats while slave morality applies to everyone</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Master morality is concerned with actions while slave morality is concerned with character</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Master morality starts with a yes to itself and derives 'bad' afterward. Slave morality starts with a no to the other, 'evil,' and defines 'good' as a reaction against it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The difference is about which judgment comes first. Ask whether each morality begins by affirming itself or by negating its enemy.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> Nietzsche uses the French term ressentiment rather than "resentment" to describe:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">The specific feeling of guilt that Kant associates with violations of duty</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">A chronic, festering hostility in those who cannot act directly on it, which turns inward and eventually generates a compensatory moral system</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">The justified grievance of the oppressed against their oppressors</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">A temporary anger that motivates immediate action</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Ressentiment is hostility that cannot be discharged in action. It festers, turns inward, and eventually becomes creative, giving birth to a new morality.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Nietzsche uses the French word for something more specific than ordinary resentment. Ask what happens to hostility that cannot be acted on.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> According to Nietzsche, the "slave revolt in morality" accomplished which of the following?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It replaced religious morality with a purely secular, rationalist ethics</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It restored the original meaning of "good" as utility for the community</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">It inverted the value system of the powerful, redefining strength and vitality as "evil" and weakness and humility as "good," and this inversion eventually came to dominate Western moral consciousness</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It gave the weak military and political power over the strong</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The slave revolt revalued values. What the nobles called good, strength and pride, became evil, and what they looked down on, weakness and humility, became good. That inversion came to dominate Western morality.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The slave revolt was a revolt in values, not in military or political power. Ask what it did to the meaning of 'good' and 'evil.'</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> Nietzsche associates the historical origin of the slave revolt in morality primarily with:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The Enlightenment philosophers, especially Kant and Rousseau</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The Industrial Revolution and the emergence of the working class</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">The priestly class within the Jewish tradition, whose values were then spread by Christianity throughout the Roman Empire</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The ancient Greeks and their democratic reforms</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Nietzsche traces the slave revolt to the priestly class of ancient Israel, whose revaluation was carried by Christianity throughout the Roman world.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Nietzsche places the slave revolt in the ancient world, long before the Enlightenment or industrialization. Revisit the First Treatise on the role of the priestly caste.</div>
  </div>
</div>

